{"theorems":[{"slogan_id":7452320,"theorem_id":22536602,"name":"Theorem 3.3","body":"Let $f$ be a real-valued function on $\\partial \\Gamma $, summing to zero on each connected component of $\\Gamma $. Then there is a voltage function $\\phi $ and a compatible current function $\\iota $ with $f$ as the boundary current function. Moreover, given two such solutions $\\phi _1$ and $\\phi _2$, $\\phi _1 - \\phi _2$ is constant on each connected component of $\\Gamma $.","slogan":"A real-valued function f on the boundary of a graph Gamma, summing to zero on each connected component, ensures the existence of a voltage function and a compatible current function with f as the boundary current. Any two such voltage solutions differ by a constant on each connected component of the graph.","theorem_type":"theorem","label":null,"link":null,"paper":{"paper_id":"1203.4045v1","source":"arXiv","title":"Circular Planar Resistor Networks with Nonlinear and Signed Conductors","authors":["Will Johnson"],"link":"http://arxiv.org/abs/1203.4045v1","summary":null,"journal_ref":null,"primary_category":"math.CO","categories":["math.CO"],"citations":3,"year":2012,"journal_published":false},"similarity":0.7211501764565614,"score":0.7211501764565614,"has_metadata":true},{"slogan_id":10817861,"theorem_id":26077729,"name":"Lemma 2.1","body":"If $\\bm {i}^{\\mathsf{T} }\\bm {1}=0$, then (\\ref {kirch1}) has a unique solution for $\\bm {v}$ up to the fundamental solution term $\\bm {1}$. That is, for each current vector $\\bm {i}$, the potential difference between nodes $a$ and $b$ is unique.","slogan":"If the sum of the current vector components is zero, then Kirchhoff's current law equation has a unique solution for the voltage vector, except for an additive constant vector. This constant vector corresponds to a uniform potential shift across all nodes. As a result, the potential difference between any two specific nodes is uniquely determined.","theorem_type":"lemma","label":null,"link":null,"paper":{"paper_id":"2502.19720v2","source":"arXiv","title":"Performance bound analysis of linear consensus algorithm on strongly connected graphs using effective resistance and reversiblization","authors":["Takumi Yonaiyama","Kazuhiro Sato"],"link":"http://arxiv.org/abs/2502.19720v2","summary":null,"journal_ref":null,"primary_category":"math.OC","categories":["math.OC","cs.MA"],"citations":0,"year":2025,"journal_published":false},"similarity":0.6847024576099952,"score":0.6847024576099952,"has_metadata":true}]}